# X++ Script to Create a Custom REST Service in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations

Below is a complete example of how to create a custom REST service in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations (D365 F&O) using X++.

## 1. Create a Data Contract Class

First, create a data contract class to define the structure of your request/response data:

```xpp
[DataContractAttribute]
class MyCustomRestDataContract
{
    str customerAccount;
    str customerName;
    str email;
    real creditLimit;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the customer account
/// </summary>
[DataMemberAttribute('CustomerAccount')]
public str parmCustomerAccount(str _customerAccount = customerAccount)
{
    customerAccount = _customerAccount;
    return customerAccount;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the customer name
/// </summary>
[DataMemberAttribute('CustomerName')]
public str parmCustomerName(str _customerName = customerName)
{
    customerName = _customerName;
    return customerName;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the email
/// </summary>
[DataMemberAttribute('Email')]
public str parmEmail(str _email = email)
{
    email = _email;
    return email;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the credit limit
/// </summary>
[DataMemberAttribute('CreditLimit')]
public real parmCreditLimit(real _creditLimit = creditLimit)
{
    creditLimit = _creditLimit;
    return creditLimit;
}
```

## 2. Create a Service Class

Next, create the service class that will contain your business logic:

```xpp
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
class MyCustomRestService
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Gets customer information by account number
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_customerAccount">Customer account number</param>
    /// <returns>Customer data contract</returns>
    public MyCustomRestDataContract getCustomerInfo(str _customerAccount)
    {
        MyCustomRestDataContract dataContract = new MyCustomRestDataContract();
        CustTable custTable;

        select firstOnly custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _customerAccount;

        if (custTable)
        {
            dataContract.parmCustomerAccount(custTable.AccountNum);
            dataContract.parmCustomerName(custTable.name());
            dataContract.parmEmail(custTable.email());
            dataContract.parmCreditLimit(custTable.CreditMax);
        }
        else
        {
            throw error(strFmt("Customer %1 not found", _customerAccount));
        }

        return dataContract;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Updates customer information
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_dataContract">Customer data contract</param>
    /// <returns>Updated customer data contract</returns>
    public MyCustomRestDataContract updateCustomerInfo(MyCustomRestDataContract _dataContract)
    {
        CustTable custTable;
        ttsBegin;

        select forUpdate custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _dataContract.parmCustomerAccount();

        if (custTable)
        {
            custTable.Name = _dataContract.parmCustomerName();
            custTable.email = _dataContract.parmEmail();
            custTable.CreditMax = _dataContract.parmCreditLimit();
            custTable.update();
        }
        else
        {
            throw error(strFmt("Customer %1 not found", _dataContract.parmCustomerAccount()));
        }

        ttsCommit;

        return this.getCustomerInfo(_dataContract.parmCustomerAccount());
    }
}
```

## 3. Create a Service Group

Create a service group to expose your service:

```xpp
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
class MyCustomRestServiceGroup
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Gets customer information
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_customerAccount">Customer account number</param>
    /// <returns>Customer data contract</returns>
    public MyCustomRestDataContract getCustomerInfo(str _customerAccount)
    {
        MyCustomRestService service = new MyCustomRestService();
        return service.getCustomerInfo(_customerAccount);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Updates customer information
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_dataContract">Customer data contract</param>
    /// <returns>Updated customer data contract</returns>
    public MyCustomRestDataContract updateCustomerInfo(MyCustomRestDataContract _dataContract)
    {
        MyCustomRestService service = new MyCustomRestService();
        return service.updateCustomerInfo(_dataContract);
    }
}
```

## 4. Register the Service in the AOT

1. In Visual Studio, go to the AOT
2. Right-click on **Service Groups** and select **New Service Group**
3. Name it "MyCustomRestServiceGroup"
4. Right-click on the new service group and select **Properties**
5. Set the **Class** property to "MyCustomRestServiceGroup"
6. Right-click on the service group and select **New Service**
7. Name it "MyCustomRestService"
8. Set the **Class** property to "MyCustomRestService"

## 5. Deploy the Service

1. Build your project
2. Right-click on the service group and select **Deploy Service Group**
3. After deployment, go to **System administration > Setup > Services and Application Integration Framework > Inbound ports**
4. Create a new inbound port:
   - Name: MyCustomRestServicePort
   - Description: Custom REST service for customer operations
   - Adapter: REST
   - URI: /api/services/MyCustomRestServiceGroup/MyCustomRestService
   - Service contract: MyCustomRestServiceGroup
5. Activate the port

## 6. Test the Service

You can now test your REST service using tools like Postman or cURL:

### GET Request Example:
```
GET https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/MyCustomRestServiceGroup/MyCustomRestService/getCustomerInfo?customerAccount=US-001
Headers:
Authorization: Bearer {your_access_token}
Accept: application/json
```

### POST Request Example:
```
POST https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/MyCustomRestServiceGroup/MyCustomRestService/updateCustomerInfo
Headers:
Authorization: Bearer {your_access_token}
Accept: application/json
Content-Type: application/json

Body:
{
    "CustomerAccount": "US-001",
    "CustomerName": "Updated Customer Name",
    "Email": "updated@customer.com",
    "CreditLimit": 10000
}
```

## Additional Notes

1. **Authentication**: You'll need to authenticate using OAuth 2.0. Register an application in Azure AD and use the client credentials flow to get an access token.

2. **Error Handling**: The example includes basic error handling. You may want to enhance it with more detailed error messages.

3. **Logging**: Consider adding logging to track service usage and errors.

4. **Security**: Ensure proper security is implemented, especially for write operations.

5. **Documentation**: Document your API endpoints and data contracts for consumers.

